We need to create a bridge between Ethereum and another blockchain
called Acki Nacki.

The architecture is as follows:
there are four modules:
- Ethereum frontend (written in Rust)
- Ethereum contract (written in Solidity)
- Acki Nacki frontend (written in Rust)
- Acki Nacki contract (written in Acki Nacki's version of Solidity)

We are responsible for the first two modules.
The Acki Nacki modules will be implemented by the Acki Nacki team.

The bridge will be a simple 1-to-1 token bridge.

Deposit:
- Initiated by the user of Ethereum frontend
- Frontend selects two random numbers
(called withdrawal hash and nullifier), concatecates them and 
calculates their hash (it's interesting to think about hash function to
be used, probably ZK-friendly Poseidon is a proper choice)
- Then these two numbers are sent to the contract together with the
deposit itself
- The contract adds this hash into incremental Merkle Tree
(a la Tornado Cash), stores the deposit in so called treasury
and emits the event with:
    - hash been received
    - concatenated hash of hash been received with deposit amount
- Frontend:
    - creates ZK proof (PLONK) that he knows:
        - the preimage of the withdrawal hash
        - amount in the concatenated hash
        - public inputs are:
            - amount
    - sends this proof to the Acki Nacki contract:
        - while Acki Nacki contratcs are not ready, interface
        IAckiNacki must be implemented and used, for the testing
        purposes some dummy implementation must be provided
        - the sending process is async, so the user must be able to
        check the status of his deposit, the event is emitted
        when the deposit is added to the Merkle Tree, but the
        transaction to the Acki Nacki contract may fail for some reason
        and the user must be able to retry it.
        - also some abstract trait for sending transaction to Acki Nacki
        must be created and used, so the implementation can be swapped
        for the testing purposes

Withdrawal:
- Initiated by the user of Acki Nacki frontend
- Ethereum Frontend creates ZK proof that he knows:
    - the preimage of the withdrawal hash
    - the nullifier
    - the Merkle proof of the inclusion of the hash in the Merkle Tree
    - the Burn transaction indeed happened on Acki Nacki
    - public inputs are:
        - amount
        - Merkle root
    - while details of the Burn transaction proof are not yet known,
    some placeholder must be used, so the trait/abstract class for it
    must be created and used with some dummy implementation for the
    testing purposes
- Ethereum contract:
    - receives the proof from the frontend
    - verifies it
    - if the proof is valid, sends the tokens to the user
    - adds the nullifier to the list of used nullifiers to prevent double
    spending

    The system must be secure against the following attacks:
    - the user tries to withdraw without burning the tokens on Acki Nacki
    - the user tries to withdraw with the same nullifier twice
    - the user tries to withdraw with the different nullifier but the same
    preimage of the withdrawal hash

    The system must be covered by the tests.
    Use mocks for the Acki Nacki contract. Merkle tree must be tested
    thoroughly. The security properties of the system must be tested
    formally using the model checker. Merkle tree must be fully
    implemented.

    Regarding tests: do not try to be too smart, just use the standard
    approach of the industry. If something is not clear, ask for
    clarifications. Do not try to implement something that is not
    explicitly required. Do not try to cheat the tests to make them
    passed. Instead, put all the failing tests into three different
    buckets:
    - Definitely broken tests
    - Unclear, bugs in tests or in the code
    - Definetely bugs

And, while write tests, consider youself as a separate agent from
the code writer.

Also don't forget to use the latest tools and best practices of the
industry. For example, use Rust 2021 edition, not 2018. Use the latest
version of the language. Use the latest version of the compiler. Use
the latest version of the standard library. Use the latest version of
the ecosystem tools.

Also don't forget to update README.md and add thoroughful
documentation for the code.